{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Parallel Break and Stop.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Parallel Break and Stop.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"parallel break and stop parallel break and stop","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Parallel Break and Stop.md","fileName":"Parallel Break and Stop.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Parallel Break and Stop","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 00:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[.NET Data Parallelism and PLINQ]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Data Parallelism and PLINQ","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Parallel Break and Stop.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Parallel Break and Stop.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"parallel break and stop parallel break and stop","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Parallel Break and Stop.md","fileName":"Parallel Break and Stop.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Parallel Break and Stop","heading":"Parallel Break and Stop","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"`ParallelLoopState.Break` says that iterations beyond a relevant index need not begin, preserving a lowest-break notion for ordered index work. `Stop` instead says that no additional iterations are useful, without defining an ordered boundary.\n\nNeither operation rewinds iterations that are already executing. Selecting between them depends on whether earlier indexes retain semantic importance, and loop bodies must tolerate a short period in which workers react to the termination request at different times.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Data Parallelism and PLINQ","hands-onparallelprogrammingwithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Parallel Break and Stop.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Note